<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="chp-for-while-loops" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>For &amp; While Loops</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			In the previous subsection, <pf>if</pf>-statements allowed your code to choose between different paths based on true/false conditions. Now we shift to a different kind of flow control: <term>repetition</term>. Loops let you repeat blocks of code multiple times, either for a fixed number of iterations or until a condition changes.
		</p>

		<p>
			MATLAB provides two primary looping structures:
			<dl width="narrow">
				<li><title>for-loops</title>
					<p>
						Repeat a fixed number of times or once for each value in a list.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li><title>while-loops</title>
					<p>
						Repeat as long as a condition remains true.
					</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<xi:include href="sec-for-loops.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="sec-while-loops.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="sec-continue-break-return.ptx" />

	<conclusion>
		<p>
			<objectives xml:id="takeaways-looping-statements">
				<ul>
					<li>
						Using <pf>for</pf> loops to iterate over sequences of numbers.
					</li>
					<li>
						Using <pf>while</pf> loops to repeat actions until a condition is met.
					</li>
					<li>
						Using <pf>break</pf>, <pf>continue</pf>, and <pf>return</pf> statements in loops (see <xref ref="sec-continue-break-return"/>).
					</li>
				</ul>
			</objectives>
		</p>
	</conclusion>

</chapter>
